[{:db/id 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-befb255b-f1bd-5c00-bc58-981c00e76ebb" "question-82f2815d-a760-5a7e-b3a4-18ea182996e3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192512 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I  represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of  $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Diagram II  represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is also a subspace of  $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin ![](images/45/4578110804bb0edbf2816e89b8231affe683257d5f37c1844294bb714f36a417.svg)  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192511 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under scalar multiplication. For example, the vector ![](images/6d/6da77166b57246e3d4bb076af75c54a10350678986adfb6e31125e0ef614bacc.svg) belongs to $A,$ but the vector ![](images/9c/9cfd2972828a5c45bc58b434debbcf4a96c2cab14daa36a1e07fc37011682960.svg) does not lie in $A$ (because it does not satisfy $x^2=y$).\n\n- The set $B$ represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have\n![](images/83/83ecb68263c21fca495034a2f1678dced07ddf9f5167c5a0a9c7703657530c47.svg)\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3-t=0 \\\\ -3+t=0 \\\\ -6+2t=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $t=3$ is the solution to the three equations. So, if we choose $t=3,$ then we find that $\\mathbf{0} \\in B.$ Therefore, $B$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-befb255b-f1bd-5c00-bc58-981c00e76ebb" :question/id #uuid "befb255b-f1bd-5c00-bc58-981c00e76ebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-35574" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d5d9586684c8606ca0f6df8dd70d9828ea04d3d9097b5a155ab66bcd7300ffd.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace.  In this case we also have that the vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6d1cab38-e87e-597f-87e0-47535946af67"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6d1cab38-e87e-597f-87e0-47535946af67" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d1cab38-e87e-597f-87e0-47535946af67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-70ea133b-bffa-5ff6-b9b3-2c01068c74c9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cd6b9cf4-8a14-5d9c-8580-90b1f71a942c" "answer-4c5b8efe-00bb-54bf-84b6-0c9423e627f4" "answer-368e8346-dba0-5eab-bd72-0c8e01860bad" "answer-99103a99-9e82-5c24-8f3c-8db058da3029" "answer-70ea133b-bffa-5ff6-b9b3-2c01068c74c9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd6b9cf4-8a14-5d9c-8580-90b1f71a942c" :answer/id #uuid "cd6b9cf4-8a14-5d9c-8580-90b1f71a942c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c5b8efe-00bb-54bf-84b6-0c9423e627f4" :answer/id #uuid "4c5b8efe-00bb-54bf-84b6-0c9423e627f4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-368e8346-dba0-5eab-bd72-0c8e01860bad" :answer/id #uuid "368e8346-dba0-5eab-bd72-0c8e01860bad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99103a99-9e82-5c24-8f3c-8db058da3029" :answer/id #uuid "99103a99-9e82-5c24-8f3c-8db058da3029" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70ea133b-bffa-5ff6-b9b3-2c01068c74c9" :answer/id #uuid "70ea133b-bffa-5ff6-b9b3-2c01068c74c9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-82f2815d-a760-5a7e-b3a4-18ea182996e3" :question/id #uuid "82f2815d-a760-5a7e-b3a4-18ea182996e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-74214" :question/problem "![](images/42/42e0a9a9e987c1c56a335f29cf939e4f03d496fad148f148c32377893dbe7c13.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.  \n\n- Diagram I is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5c15890f-719b-5cd8-8053-341247002966"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5c15890f-719b-5cd8-8053-341247002966" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c15890f-719b-5cd8-8053-341247002966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f16a724b-c1f8-5ad0-8225-b9af9742e1cc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-baba48d0-d9fb-5842-84e6-6919f8e36cee" "answer-f16a724b-c1f8-5ad0-8225-b9af9742e1cc" "answer-dbd0836e-e6f4-5b58-bfb5-c8dec7e9a94d" "answer-fe963c24-be42-5cfe-a77e-8b918c0ffa49" "answer-f67abfd4-642f-5614-876f-390eb3d1595b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-baba48d0-d9fb-5842-84e6-6919f8e36cee" :answer/id #uuid "baba48d0-d9fb-5842-84e6-6919f8e36cee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f16a724b-c1f8-5ad0-8225-b9af9742e1cc" :answer/id #uuid "f16a724b-c1f8-5ad0-8225-b9af9742e1cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbd0836e-e6f4-5b58-bfb5-c8dec7e9a94d" :answer/id #uuid "dbd0836e-e6f4-5b58-bfb5-c8dec7e9a94d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe963c24-be42-5cfe-a77e-8b918c0ffa49" :answer/id #uuid "fe963c24-be42-5cfe-a77e-8b918c0ffa49" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f67abfd4-642f-5614-876f-390eb3d1595b" :answer/id #uuid "f67abfd4-642f-5614-876f-390eb3d1595b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193883 :tutorial/content "In $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n*The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ are straight lines that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows a subspace in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c0ea9584ae3229a7930c6ef2b53031770a7190b980b5b595cc233cae29ea49d.png)\n\nLet's now consider a few examples of subsets of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that are *not* subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\n![](images/5a/5aac9808e9196653ee54974b495443a95d8913cd0b220a6893acb68ff3b967bb.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is *not* a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II is not a subspace because $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf v$ belong to the set while $\\mathbf u+\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under vector addition.\n\n- Diagram III is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $-\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193884 :tutorial/content "In $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n*The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ are straight lines or planes that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/57/57b6625b607537d1c90e849d6ee6ac8480648aa4ad6e3a357bedc8d8408256ad.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the sets shown in the diagrams below are *not* subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4fbaee302e1c25e740f4164841a674243a43197b0f20ddb35d2d41bb4175b68.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a plane that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192926 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
